steps.body:
Put a union inside a template literal type, and you get one string for each member. With two unions, you get every combination:

```typescript types
type Entity = "order" | "user";
type Action = "created" | "deleted";
type EventName = `${Entity}.${Action}`;
//   ^? type EventName = "order.created" | "order.deleted" | "user.created" | "user.deleted"
```

Two members times two members makes four names. Add a third entity, and its two names join the union on their own.

TypeScript also has four built-in types that change the case of string types: `Uppercase`, `Lowercase`, `Capitalize` (the first letter only), and `Uncapitalize`. They're how libraries derive names like `onClick` from `click`:

```typescript types
type Method = Uppercase<"get" | "post">;
//   ^? type Method = "GET" | "POST"
type Handler = `on${Capitalize<"click" | "submit">}`;
//   ^? type Handler = "onClick" | "onSubmit"
```

They only change types. To change the case of a value at run time, you still call a method like `toUpperCase()`.

examples.code:
const ENTITIES = ["order", "user"] as const;
const ACTIONS = ["created", "deleted"] as const;

type Entity = (typeof ENTITIES)[number];
type Action = (typeof ACTIONS)[number];
type EventName = `${Entity}.${Action}`;
//   ^? type EventName = "order.created" | "order.deleted" | "user.created" | "user.deleted"

function emit(event: EventName, id: string): string {
  return `[${event}] ${id}`;
}

const all: EventName[] = [];
for (const entity of ENTITIES) {
  for (const action of ACTIONS) {
    all.push(`${entity}.${action}`);
  }
}

console.log(`${all.length} events: ${all.join(", ")}`);
console.log(emit("order.created", "ord_1001"));
console.log(emit("user.deleted", "usr_7"));

examples.explanation:
`Entity` and `Action` come from the two `as const` lists, and `EventName` combines them, so the bubble lists all four names. The loops build the same four strings at run time. `all.push` expects an `EventName`, so each template string is checked against the pattern. A misspelled event is caught where you use it:

```typescript error
type EventName = `${"order" | "user"}.${"created" | "deleted"}`;

function emit(event: EventName, id: string): string {
  return `[${event}] ${id}`;
}

console.log(emit("order.updated", "ord_1001"));
```

examples.code:
type Getter<K extends string> = `get${Capitalize<K>}`;

type UserField = "name" | "email";
type UserGetter = Getter<UserField>;
//   ^? type UserGetter = "getEmail" | "getName"

const user = { name: "Ada", email: "ada@example.com" };

const getters: Record<UserGetter, () => string> = {
  getName: () => user.name,
  getEmail: () => user.email,
};

console.log(getters.getName());
console.log(getters.getEmail());

examples.explanation:
`Getter` is a generic type: give it field names, and it returns getter names, with each field's first letter capitalized. `K extends string` is required, because `Capitalize` only takes string types. `Record<UserGetter, () => string>` then needs exactly one function per name, so a missing or misspelled getter doesn't compile:

```typescript error
type UserGetter = `get${Capitalize<"name" | "email">}`;

const getters: Record<UserGetter, () => string> = {
  getName: () => "Ada",
  getemail: () => "ada@example.com",
};
console.log(getters.getName());
```

The checker reports TS2561 and asks whether you meant `getEmail`.

